CAT = "nlvcaSafeWork"; SEG = "SWK"
# SSVV VCA-ETT 3.0 (2026), A.02 Working safely, consultation and inspections (A.02.01.01 safe
# behaviour, alcohol, drugs and medicines, good housekeeping; A.02.03.01 work meeting and toolbox
# meeting) and A.03 Prevention (unsafe actions and situations, prevention hierarchy, RI&E, TRA,
# A.03.03.01 LMRA). Toetsmatrijs 2026: 4 of 40 per exam (A.02 2, A.03 2).
Q = [
("Which behaviour is typical of working safely?",
 "Speaking up about unsafe acts", ["Working on when very tired", "Leaving defects for others to spot", "Keeping near misses to yourself"],
 "Safe behaviour includes following instructions, calling others to account for unsafe actions and reporting unsafe situations. Keeping quiet about a near miss throws away a warning."),
("What does alcohol do to a worker's view of their own abilities?",
 "Leads to overestimating them", ["Makes them more cautious", "Has no effect in small amounts", "Sharpens risk judgement"],
 "Alcohol and drugs reduce alertness and judgement and lead people to overestimate what they can do. Even small amounts affect safe working."),
("Your new medicine may cause drowsiness and you have high-risk work today. What should you do?",
 "Ask a doctor if the work is safe", ["Halve the dose on work days", "Drink coffee to stay alert", "Do the work and take extra breaks"],
 "Some medicines act like drugs. The ETT says to consult a doctor about whether high-risk work can be done while taking them, not to change the dose yourself."),
("A worker knows they have a persistent drinking problem. What is expected of them?",
 "Seek help and avoid risky work", ["Drink only during breaks", "Keep it hidden from the supervisor", "Switch to night shifts"],
 "With problematic use, the worker informs the supervisor, keeps away from hazardous work that could endanger others and seeks help. Hiding it puts colleagues at risk."),
("Which is an example of good housekeeping on site?",
 "Hanging cables on safety hooks", ["Stacking waste by the exit", "Leaving tools where last used", "Running hoses across walkways"],
 "Good housekeeping means clearing up, storing materials and suspending or routing cables safely, for example on safety hooks. Waste by an exit blocks an escape route."),
("Who carries out the last minute risk analysis (LMRA)?",
 "Worker doing the job", ["Safety advisor in the office", "Client's site owner", "Inspector on a visit"],
 "The LMRA is done by the operational employees themselves at the workplace, just before starting, sometimes with a checklist. It cannot be delegated to someone away from the job."),
("Who chairs the meeting between the employer and the employee representation?",
 "Employer", ["Prevention advisor", "Labour inspector", "Employee delegate"],
 "In that meeting the employer chairs, with delegations of employers and employees and experts such as prevention advisors. The prevention advisor attends as an expert."),
("Which is a feature of a good work meeting?",
 "Agenda and written minutes", ["Held only after accidents", "Chaired by an inspector", "One-way briefing, no questions"],
 "A work meeting is held regularly, is structured with an agenda and minutes, is interactive and checks that information has been understood."),
("What is the main aim of a toolbox meeting?",
 "Prevent unsafe behaviour", ["Assign next week's tasks", "Discuss pay and bonuses", "Train staff for a diploma"],
 "A toolbox meeting is a short, informal HSE meeting to inform and motivate employees to avoid unsafe actions and unsafe behaviour."),
("Which topic suits a toolbox meeting?",
 "Lessons from a recent near miss", ["The company's annual profit figures", "Tender prices for a new project", "Staff holiday planning"],
 "Toolbox subjects include housekeeping, working methods, emergency procedures, incidents, inspection findings and PPE. Business figures do not belong there."),
("How should the leader close a toolbox meeting?",
 "Check the message was understood", ["Hand out a written test", "Skip questions to save time", "Only read the minutes aloud"],
 "Key points of a toolbox meeting are clear agreements recorded in the minutes and checking that participants understood the message."),
("Which of these is an unsafe situation rather than an unsafe action?",
 "Blocked escape route", ["Skipping prescribed gloves", "Bypassing a safety switch", "Using a broken hammer"],
 "An unsafe situation is a condition, such as poor light, a blocked escape route or an unguarded machine. Skipping PPE, bypassing safeguards and using broken tools are unsafe actions."),
("Which of these is an unsafe action?",
 "Starting without a work permit", ["Poor lighting in the main stairwell", "Untidy floor in a workshop", "Unguarded machine shaft"],
 "Working without the agreed permit or safety agreements is an unsafe action by a person. Poor light, untidy floors and missing guards are unsafe situations."),
("Which step comes first in the prevention hierarchy?",
 "Remove the hazard at source", ["Hand out suitable PPE", "Rotate staff on the task", "Fit a collective guard rail"],
 "The hierarchy runs source measures, collective measures, individual measures, then PPE. Removing the cause or choosing a safer method always comes first."),
("A noisy machine cannot be made quieter. What is the next step in the hierarchy?",
 "Enclose the noisy machine", ["Issue earmuffs to all staff", "Rotate the operators", "Put up warning signs"],
 "When the source cannot be removed, collective measures follow, such as shielding or enclosing. Job rotation is an individual measure and earmuffs are PPE, the last step."),
("Where does job rotation sit in the prevention hierarchy?",
 "Individual measures", ["Source measures", "Collective measures", "Personal protection"],
 "Job rotation, spreading demanding work, instruction and supervision are individual measures, step 3. PPE is step 4."),
("In a risk assessment and evaluation (RI&E), what follows identifying the hazards?",
 "Assessing the risks", ["Choosing the PPE", "Evaluating remaining risks", "Writing work instructions"],
 "The RI&E steps are: identify hazards, assess risks, evaluate risks, determine measures, evaluate the remaining risks."),
("What is a task risk analysis (TRA) used for?",
 "Agreeing controls for a risky task", ["Ranking workers by experience", "Pricing a maintenance contract", "Scheduling the toolbox meetings"],
 "A TRA analyses and evaluates the risks of a high-risk task, or a task in a high-risk area, so that control measures can be agreed before it starts."),
("On what does the employer base the work instructions for high-risk tasks?",
 "RI&E and task risk analysis", ["Workers' personal preferences", "Supplier price lists", "Last year's sick leave"],
 "The employer records high-risk tasks and prepares work instructions on the basis of the RI&E and the TRA."),
("When should you carry out an LMRA?",
 "Before any task, routine too", ["Only when you are new on site", "Once a week on Mondays", "Only after an incident"],
 "The LMRA is done before starting work or a new task, when conditions change and also for routine work."),
("Conditions change halfway through a job. What does that mean for your LMRA?",
 "Carry out a new one", ["It still applies", "The supervisor does it", "Wait until the next day"],
 "A change in working conditions is one of the moments for an LMRA. The earlier analysis no longer covers the new situation."),
("Your LMRA shows the escape route is blocked. Which follow-up action fits?",
 "Hold off and report the finding", ["Start and stay vigilant", "Start and wear extra PPE", "Note it for next week's meeting"],
 "LMRA follow-up actions are start or do not start, stop, consult, adjust the method, report findings and stay alert. Starting with no escape route accepts the risk instead of removing it."),
]
